Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  all right breaking news  pharmaceutical giant pfizer just  announced moments ago that its  coronavirus vaccine is  95 effective a second vaccine is now  on the horizon it could eventually free  us from this pandemic  developed in record time scientists  raced to free the world from the grasp  of covid19  but just as vaccines started rolling out  new threats emerged  casting a shadow of doubt there is a  realistic possibility  that this variant may be associated with  increased mortality  public health officials are trying to  contain an outbreak of one of the more  contagious  new variants they go by names like b117  b1351 and p1  what is becoming abundantly clear is  these new variants  are more infectious spread more easily  and can trigger  deadly outbreaks  virus mutation is normal but near the  end of 2020  southeast england became a hot spot for  a new coronavirus variant that started  to spread rapidly  it had 23 mutations  for scientists at the university of  cambridge in the uk  the search was on we didn't know that  you could find  viruses with so many mutations in one  virus  in the hunt for answers world-renowned  virologist ravi gupta has two roles  one in the lab at cambridge and the  other down the road and adam brooks  hospital  it was here he saw a cancer patient who  held important clues  the person was affected with the  coronavirus but because his immune  system was weakened by chemotherapy he  couldn't get rid of it  he was sick for more than a hundred days  allowing the virus to replicate  again and again we'd been learning for  a year that this virus didn't change it  was always the same there were a few  mutations here and there  and yet in this person we saw just the  most dramatic shifts you could imagine  a virus's strategy for survival is to  multiply by making copies of itself  but the copies aren't always identical  as sars cov2 replicates  small copying mistakes known as  mutations can happen  on average a strain if this particular  coronavirus can produce  one or two random mutations per month  some  have an advantage over others helping it  survive others don't  these mutations when combined with  natural selection  allow viruses to quickly adapt and  increase the likelihood of being passed  on to the next generation  while mutations that make the virus less  fit are likely  to die out in the case of the b117  variant found in the uk  many researchers believed that it  evolved within a single  immunocompromised patient  people with weakened immune systems can  have trouble fighting and killing the  virus  allowing it to continuously replicate  for weeks or months  generating more and more mutations it's  just a race against who can develop  quicker the virus or  us stephen kemp is a research associate  at university college london  he worked with gupta tracking the  evolution of the virus in that cancer  patient  who became chronically infected with the  coronavirus  he was then transferred to our hospital  where he stayed in our care for  about 100 days and what we did was over  those hundred days  at sequential time point we took various  different samples  and what we noticed was the patient was  developing a number of different  mutations  for example around day 66 after treating  the patient with two doses of  convalescent plasma  the virus's genetic structure started  shifting  it seems like after he'd received a  specific therapy which is called  convalescent plasma which is  plasma that's been taken from patients  who have defeated covid  when they're given to this patient it  seems that a number of mutations arose  but one of the mutations we found led us  to discover the b117 variant because  this mutation in our patient that had  emerged in response to treatment  actually was one of the key mutations  found in the b117  that key mutation is now known to make  the variant more  infectious there's not a whole lot of  evidence to suggest that it's  more deadly just that it's more  transmissible we were the first to  actually show that in vitro that these  mutations actually had a functional  impact  and therefore this was a sign that the  virus was not only evolving but actually  doing things to overcome  treatments that we were throwing at the  virus and this is the virus spike  protein here in  blue and it just shows the interaction  and how the virus is binding  so we went from looking at one patient  and then trying to understand how common  was this mutation  in coronaviruses from around the world  and that's where we found that there was  this big cluster of  hundreds of sequences from patients in  the uk that not only had our mutation of  interest  but had seven other spike mutations and  this was unprecedented to see  chronic infections are rare but there  are a growing number of cases around the  world  showing evidence of immunocompromised  covid19 patients  accelerating viral evolution i tested  positive for  like a total of nine times that's brutal  that's almost  like two months right of testing  positive and not going anywhere doing  groceries online not seeing anyone  literally just being in our house it was  the start of the pandemic  and ottawa resident brianne quarle  couldn't catch her breath  my youngest daughter had a birthday in  march and i was making her cake and  normally  i love to bake love to cook and i can do  it standing no problem  but i found myself taking breaks and  having to sit down to ice her cake and i  said to my husband i'm like oh thank god  i can sit down to do  this i'm so tired he was like well  that's weird  the mother of three decided to go to the  hospital and in a 24-hour span  coral went from needing an oxygen mask  to being intubated  she spent the next two weeks on a  ventilator  have you ever felt anything in your life  like you did  when you had covered 19 and that  struggle to breathe what was that like  no i've never felt anything that bad in  my life the struggle to breathe was  scary because  i've always had this irrational fear you  know of drowning or suffocating  so when i couldn't get my breath it felt  like that's what it would be like  to drown or to suffocate but coral's  health struggle started long before the  pandemic  in 2018 doctors found a brain tumor a  year later she was diagnosed with two  autoimmune disorders the 41 year old  falls into a category of people  who have a compromised immune system and  then  kovitt hit and that just sunk the boat  even further  immunocompromised people were not  included in the covid19 vaccine trials  so there's very little data on the risks  to them  it's currently unknown whether they'll  even be able to have an immune response  if they do get the shot what do we know  about what happens to them when they get  the vaccine  i think if you have an immune  compromising condition you are exactly  the population we want to get vaccinated  and i encourage you to do so  dr suman chakrabarti is an infectious  disease specialist at trillium health  partners in mississauga ontario  the really important thing if you look  at a place like israel is that we're  seeing  real world evidence that the vaccine is  very effective and this is a population  including young people  older people as well as people with  impaired immune systems  new research on variants of undercut but  not eliminated hope that covid19  injections  will end the pandemic a report released  in february suggested the pfizer buy in  tech vaccine  can still protect against new variants  but the company noted its shot is a  little less protective against the  variant in south africa  which may be able to escape antibodies  what is the research showing about how  effective these vaccines are  against the variants so if you look in  in certain kind of test tube experiments  you'll see that the variant from south  africa for example does have a reduced  response to the vaccines especially  astrazeneca but if you look more closely  you still have way more antibody than  you need to neutralize it so  while this might be something we see in  a chest tube clinically what we see  in person the person is still able to  clear the virus enough that they don't  get severe disease  and that's what i think is the most  important metric for us to look at  so taking a vaccine saves you from dying  which is the number one reason to  have a vaccine on a personal level so  that's fantastic news and that seems to  hold true  even for some of the more worrying  variants like the south african one  we are still seeing protection from  severe disease and mortality  moderna and pfizer are already working  on booster shots against the b1351  variant  so certainly if there are any additional  mutations which  will render the vaccines ineffective  then we'll have to  pull up our bootstraps and work a bit  faster  back in ottawa high-risk patients like  brienne coral  are worried while she says she will get  the vaccine when it becomes available to  her  there is a chance that she could get  infected with one of the new variants  while waiting it's a reality coral can't  afford to become true  it's been almost a year since she got  covid19 and  she's still suffering i still have  quite a few lingering effects to be  honest i still can't taste or smell  i lost most of my hair so this is  regrowth  i'm still not where i want to be i'm  better than where i was  but i'm still not that mom that i want  to be

